export const debate = {"id":"oconnor-carter-free-will-determinism-2024","number":"153","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Alex O'Connor vs Dr Alex Carter: Free Will and Determinism","label":"Free will and compatibilism","date":"2026-08-08","duration":"1 hr 09 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CRpsJgYVl-8","motion":"Do determinism and unchosen desires eliminate free will, or can determined agents be free when acting voluntarily and without coercion?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"O'Connor pressed ultimate-source objections and practical compassion; Carter more strongly defended graded, attributable agency while preserving interpersonal and normative dimensions.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"you can do whatever you will you just can't will what you will","context":"O'Connor crystallizes his claim that acting on a desire cannot supply ultimate control when the motivating desire was not itself selected."},"con":{"text":"the cause can be roughly designated as myself","context":"Carter states the core compatibilist criterion: caused conduct may remain free when its relevant source is attributable to the acting person."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Determinist critique","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Compatibilist freedom","speaker":"Alex Carter","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":79,"con":82},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-01-control-dilemma","title":"Determination, randomness, and ultimate control","timebox":"2:58–61:51","score":{"pro":78,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-01-control-dilemma","time":"2:58","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Free will appears to require genuine authorship and the capacity to act differently, yet determination traces action to prior causes while randomness supplies no agent control.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The determined-or-random dilemma establishes a lucid, centrally relevant challenge by explaining why neither inherited causation nor sheer contingency straightforwardly supplies the authorship ordinarily associated with doing otherwise. Principal limitation: The formulation leaves genuine authorship and the pertinent alternative-possibilities requirement insufficiently specified, so its sweeping conclusion presupposes that compatibilist attributable control cannot qualify as philosophically meaningful freedom. Live burden: O'Connor must defend ultimate sourcehood as necessary, rather than merely asserting that ordinary voluntary and reasons-responsive agency fails because its causal antecedents were not independently selected by the person. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects excellent route contact and an intelligible dilemma, moderated by conceptual instability, slightly overstated warrant, and incomplete engagement with Carter's strongest criterion of attributable voluntary causation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-01-attributable-causation","time":"7:16","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Deterministic causation need not defeat freedom because compelled behavior differs meaningfully from voluntary conduct whose relevant cause can be attributed to the acting person.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: Carter directly accepts causal determination while drawing a highly responsive distinction between compulsion and voluntary action, thereby offering a coherent compatibilist criterion instead of merely denying O'Connor's causal premises. Principal limitation: Saying that a cause can be roughly designated as oneself leaves the conditions of personal attribution schematic and does not independently establish why that ordinary semantic distinction answers the stronger demand for ultimate authorship. Live burden: Carter must specify which psychological structures, reasons-responsive capacities, histories, and coercive conditions make conduct attributable to a person without circularly labeling every internally caused movement free. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional contact with both causal challenges, calibrated argumentative force, and a productive conceptual reframe, tempered only by the approximate self-attribution standard and its incomplete answer to ultimate-source concerns.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-02-substrate-neutral-dilemma","time":"41:17","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Changing the physical or mental substrate does not escape the basic dilemma: any event is either determined by antecedent conditions or undetermined and uncontrolled.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The substrate-neutral formulation broadens the causal challenge beyond any particular theory of mind, preventing movement from neural to psychological or immaterial vocabulary from silently supplying missing authorship and control. Principal limitation: Its purported exhaustiveness treats determination and randomness as settling agency without addressing whether organized, reasons-responsive causal processes can instantiate control despite every component having antecedent conditions. Live burden: O'Connor must connect the metaphysical partition to an evaluative criterion for freedom and demonstrate why higher-level agency cannot constitute a legitimate causal capacity merely because determined mechanisms realize it. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes a portable, logically economical extension of the central dilemma, while reflecting an underdefended conception of control, limited compatibilist engagement, relevant-nonanswer status, and the questionable presumption that causal explanation necessarily displaces personal-level authorship.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-02-mediopassive-agency","time":"60:59","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Some actions are simultaneously things a person does and things happening to that person, so agency need not require deliberate manufacture of its underlying processes.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The mediopassive category supplies a clear conceptual model for events that both belong to a person and happen through processes the person did not manufacture, directly challenging the assumption that agency requires total production. Principal limitation: The grammatical and marriage examples establish the intelligibility of simultaneous activity and passivity, but they do not prove that ordinary attribution answers O'Connor's separate demand for ultimate authorship. Live burden: Carter must specify when mediopassive ownership constitutes freedom, distinguish it from coercion or pathological compulsion, and explain why the relevant attribution is philosophically substantive rather than conventional. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness across sourcehood, desire, and control objections, stable exposition, proportionate force, and a memorable practical model, moderated by its incomplete refutation of the ultimate-source criterion.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-02-desires-voluntariness","title":"Unchosen desires and voluntary action","timebox":"5:21–31:41","score":{"pro":81,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-03-causal-regress-of-desire","time":"5:21","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Each desire or mental choice is either random and uncontrolled or determined through an antecedent causal chain, so neither origin grants ultimate control.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The causal regress applies the opening dilemma directly to desires and choices, clearly exposing how tracing any motivating mental event backward appears never to terminate in an independently self-created source. Principal limitation: Its categorical treatment of every mental event compresses away agent-level organization and assumes that causal dependence automatically defeats all relevant control, rather than only the especially demanding control of ultimate origination. Live burden: O'Connor must demonstrate why a determined desire cannot remain authentically attributable, responsive to reasons, and integrated with the person's enduring evaluative capacities despite having antecedent causes outside deliberate selection. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes focused central relevance and forceful explanatory continuity, while reflecting missing qualification, universal scope, and limited engagement with the strongest compatibilist distinction between ultimate sourcehood and attributable agency.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-03-degrees-of-voluntariness","time":"26:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Freedom is graded and context-sensitive: a person may stifle a sneeze but not an involuntary tic, revealing meaningful control without having chosen every desire.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The sneeze-and-tic contrast supplies a concrete, discriminating example of graded voluntariness and directly replaces the demand to choose desires with an ordinary capacity to regulate conduct under differing constraints. Principal limitation: The example establishes a practical distinction between voluntary and involuntary behavior but does not resolve whether either category possesses the ultimate sourcehood targeted by O'Connor's metaphysical argument. Live burden: Carter must clarify why these degrees deserve the contested name freedom and explain how reasons-responsiveness, inhibition, and contextual impediments jointly distinguish attributable agency from merely complex determined behavior. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, stable terminology, proportionate force, and an illuminating alternative criterion, with the remaining reservation that ordinary voluntary control can coexist with the absence of ultimate authorship and therefore partly changes the question.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-04-unchosen-wants","time":"22:01","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Choices arise from force or from wants; force plainly defeats control, while wants cannot simply be chosen, leaving their resulting actions without ultimate authorship.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The want-or-force argument directly challenges Carter's attribution criterion by demonstrating that action through personal desire does not automatically establish control over the motivational basis producing conduct. Principal limitation: The unqualified dichotomy treats control univocally, presuming freedom requires choosing the operative desire rather than acting through stable, reasons-responsive dispositions expressing the person. Live burden: O'Connor must explain why prior authorship of wanting is indispensable, and why reflective endorsement, inhibition, learning, deliberative revision, or responsiveness to reasons cannot constitute meaningful secondary self-government. Locked score: The strong competent band rewards a crisp, decisive answer to attributable causation and a memorable regress of willing, while registering universal scope, missing qualification, and insufficient charity toward the compatibilist distinction between responsibly owning a desire and independently creating it.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-04-undeliberated-voluntary-action","time":"28:52","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Ordinary actions may genuinely belong to an agent without conscious deliberation or explicit willing, because voluntary ownership need not mean deliberate manufacture of every operative desire.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move usefully separates voluntary ownership from conscious manufacture, answering the assumption that an action belongs to someone only when every underlying desire or decision was explicitly and deliberately produced. Principal limitation: The selected evidence more clearly demonstrates degrees of behavioral control than the distinctive proposition about undeliberated action, leaving that ownership claim only partially recoverable and comparatively under-supported. Live burden: Carter must identify the nonconscious capacities or integrated dispositions that make an undeliberated movement the person's action and show why such attribution is more than ordinary linguistic convenience. Locked score: The strong competent band credits close responsiveness, a plausible compatibilist distinction, and relevance to mediopassive agency, while reflecting evidentiary overlap with the sneeze example, partly unstable terminology, and the unresolved difference between attributable conduct and ultimate authorship.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-03-sourcehood-and-perspective","title":"Causal sourcehood and first-person agency","timebox":"56:32–65:15","score":{"pro":72,"con":68},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-05-unchosen-source-regress","time":"56:32","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Choices arise through unchosen upbringing, genetics, circumstances, and dispositions, so tracing their causal source backward never reaches a self-created origin of agency.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The source regress concretely traces conduct through upbringing, parents, genetics, environment, and conformity, directly challenging whether local personal attribution can terminate the explanatory search for authorship. Principal limitation: The universal behavioral claim and same-conditions counterfactual substantially exceed the illustrative evidence, while conflating absence of ultimate self-creation with absence of every meaningful personal source. Live burden: O'Connor must demonstrate why attributable sourcehood remains inadequate when character and deliberation reliably express integrated personal values, exercise ordinary capacities, and respond counterfactually to reasons. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes clear central relevance and a recognizable regress, while reflecting materially overstated certainty, missing qualification, partial responsiveness to nonreductive meaning, and inadequate engagement with the compatibilist contention that agents need not independently create themselves to act responsibly through their own developed capacities.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-05-reflexive-first-person-perspective","time":"59:47","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Disclosing a prediction to a self-aware subject may provoke its defeat, revealing a reflexive first-person perspective that ordinary third-person prediction does not capture.","score":68,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reflexive-prediction idea identifies a genuine complication for third-person modeling, because information about a forecast can enter the subject's deliberation and alter the conduct being predicted. Principal limitation: The selected passage only partially recovers that mechanism and does not show that prediction defeat escapes determination, since the subject's contrary response may itself be causally produced by receiving the prediction. Live burden: Carter must distinguish epistemic limits on prediction from metaphysical indeterminacy or agential sourcehood and directly connect first-person reflexivity to the causal regress it is meant to answer. Locked score: The mixed band credits an intriguing, cautiously presented perspective relevant to nonreductive personhood, while reflecting weak decisive contact, incomplete evidentiary recovery, unstable scope, and failure to establish that self-reference supplies freedom rather than merely complicating forecasts.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-06-introspective-mechanism-gap","time":"62:31","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Voluntary movement can occur even when a person cannot introspectively identify how wanting or deciding produces the bodily motion, as illustrated by moving a finger.","score":67,"critique":"Strongest feature: The finger-movement example vividly separates performing an apparently voluntary action from understanding the mechanism through which intention produces bodily motion, thereby generating a legitimate explanatory question about implementation. Principal limitation: Restricted introspective access does not establish absent agency or control, while the excerpt only partially recovers the wanting-to-movement gap and directly contacts no locked route requirement. Live burden: O'Connor must explain why ignorance of implementation undermines authorship rather than reflecting ordinary cognitive opacity, then connect that contention specifically to determinism or unchosen desire. Locked score: The mixed band credits exploratory calibration, an accessible phenomenological observation, and coherent local reasoning, while reflecting low route relevance, partial proposition recovery, medium evidentiary confidence, and no developed response to functional, hierarchical, or compatibilist explanations of ordinary voluntary bodily control.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-06-public-first-consciousness","time":"63:54","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Thought may be socially and behaviorally public before it is privately represented, so consciousness cannot simply be isolated as a discrete inner object through introspection.","score":69,"critique":"Strongest feature: The pain-behavior example and feedback-loop account offer meaningful adjacent evidence against treating consciousness as a wholly private, discrete internal object available through reductive inspection alone. Principal limitation: The reply shifts public and private between behavioral and epistemic senses, and it does not directly explain the mechanism connecting voluntary intention to movement or answer the regress through unchosen causes. Live burden: Carter must distinguish public evidence of experience from social or semantic constitution, then show how that distinction bears specifically on agency rather than consciousness generally. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes a recoverable and potentially fruitful public-first thesis with relevant subsidiary support, while reflecting slightly overstated breadth, unstable terminology, weak decisive responsiveness, and failure to contact the precise introspective or sourcehood demands raised by O'Connor.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-04-responsibility-and-ethical-practice","title":"Responsibility, desert, and interpersonal ethics","timebox":"32:03–48:08","score":{"pro":84,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-07-consequentialist-responsibility","time":"32:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Denying ultimate desert does not abolish social responses to wrongdoing, because prevention, deterrence, and rehabilitation can still alter the desires governing future conduct.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: O'Connor gives a substantive practical answer by explaining how deterrent consequences can reshape competing motivations, preserving prevention and rehabilitation even after ultimate moral desert is rejected. Principal limitation: This consequential account shows that useful social practices survive, but it does not exhaust responsibility's interpersonal dimensions, including answerability, recognition, apology, trust, and the moral significance of treating another person as an agent. Live burden: O'Connor must explain how forward-looking practices avoid instrumentalizing offenders and victims while retaining proportionate concern for reasons, relationships, and the meanings communicated through blame or forgiveness. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise distinctions, excellent practical relevance, explicit qualification, and a well-matched mechanism, moderated by the narrower conclusion that consequential utility can replace rather than merely supplement richer ethical conceptions of responsibility.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-07-interpersonal-responsibility","time":"37:28","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Responsibility is an interpersonal ethical practice, and freedom belongs to nuanced recognition of persons rather than an absolute, all-or-nothing metaphysical possession.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: Carter identifies a dimension omitted by pure deterrence, arguing that responsibility structures relationships of answerability and recognition through which individuals are addressed as persons rather than impersonally managed objects. Principal limitation: Semantic freedom and ethical indispensability remain elastic, because valuable interpersonal practices need not require the compatibilist label or metaphysical freedom beyond agency-sensitive consequentialism. Live burden: Carter must demonstrate what distinctive explanatory or normative work freedom performs that respect, communication, reasons-responsive treatment, and humane accountability could not perform within determinism without ultimate desert. Locked score: The strong competent band rewards an ethically serious, highly responsive reframe and lucid preservation of personal meaning, while reflecting overstated indispensability, flexible terminology, and incomplete engagement with O'Connor's contention that humane interpersonal practices remain available without ultimate responsibility.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-08-determinist-compassion","time":"46:19","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Recognizing that wrongdoers did not control every determining influence can encourage greater charity and kindness without preventing protective or corrective social action.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The compassion argument gives determinism ethical relevance, showing how attention to unchosen causal influences can temper vindictiveness while preserving protection, deterrence, rehabilitation, accountability, and interpersonal regard. Principal limitation: This suggestion cannot establish that determinist belief uniquely encourages compassion, because causal understanding might foster fatalism, technocratic management, diminished agency recognition, or inadequate attention to victims. Live burden: O'Connor must specify institutional safeguards preserving accountability, proportionality, victim recognition, and interpersonal respect while replacing retributive desert with humane forward-looking responses to harmful conduct. Locked score: The strong competent band reflects humane relevance, continuity with consequential responsibility, and appropriately conditional practical force, tempered by limited comparative support and no demonstration that metaphysical determinism reliably improves moral attitudes across social contexts.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-08-ethical-tension","time":"46:52","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Ethical judgment should retain both the picture of responsible choice and the picture of causal helplessness, rather than reducing persons to either extreme.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: Carter's plural-picture framework captures an important ethical tension, permitting judgment to acknowledge both meaningful choice and constraining causal histories without forcing every practical encounter into one reductive interpretive image. Principal limitation: The metaphor of holding multiple pictures explains flexibility but establishes neither their mutual consistency, comparative priority, nor truth, while a determinist could preserve identical plurality without accepting compatibilist freedom. Live burden: Carter must supply criteria determining when responsibility, compassion, coercion, or helplessness should predominate and show how the framework substantively constrains judgment instead of merely redescribing moral ambivalence. Locked score: The strong competent band recognizes conceptual richness, calibrated plausibility, and central relevance to nonreductive personhood, while reflecting metaphorical instability, compressed warrant, and the inability of ethical plurality alone to resolve the disputed metaphysics underlying human agency.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-05-moral-meaning","title":"Causal explanation and moral meaning","timebox":"49:55–54:29","score":{"pro":76,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-pro-09-evolved-moral-desire","time":"49:55","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Moral motivation may arise from evolved emotional desires that compete with other wants and promote cooperation, restraint, and the stability of social groups.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The evolutionary proposal supplies a naturalistic mechanism for moral motivation, connecting cooperative stability with desires that compete against destructive impulses without invoking chosen will. Principal limitation: The explanation shifts among genes, organisms, societies, intuitions, and desires, offering little comparison with cultural learning or normative reasoning and risking unsupported transitions between selection levels. Live burden: O'Connor must distinguish moral motivation's causal origin from moral judgment's content and justification, then identify evidence separating evolutionary influence from cultural and social-developmental explanations. Locked score: The strong competent band credits a relevant, plausible subsidiary explanation with appropriately tentative presentation, while reflecting explanatory compression, unstable analytical levels, limited evidence, and no demonstration that a naturalistic genealogy reduces or displaces moral desire's richer normative meaning.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"move-con-09-normative-nonreduction","time":"52:22","role":"Major direct reply","words":"An evolutionary or psychological account may explain moral motivation without exhausting moral intuition, whose normative meaning is richer than its causal origins.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: Carter precisely diagnoses a category problem by distinguishing causal psychological motivation from the richer normative content of moral intuition, thereby blocking an unwarranted inference from explanation to exhaustive reduction. Principal limitation: The reply is decisive only against a debunking or reductive interpretation, whereas O'Connor's narrower claim may concern how moral motivation arises without denying that normative discourse has additional semantic content. Live burden: Carter must establish which irreducible normative properties remain, how they guide reasons or judgment, and why causal genealogy cannot fully explain their authority rather than merely their phenomenology. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, stable distinctions, explicit qualification, and appropriately modest force, tempered by the possibility that the response targets a stronger reductionist conclusion than the naturalistic account actually requires.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":79,"strengths":["The determined-or-random framework consistently targeted the demanding notion of ultimate authorship across causes, desires, and personal history.","The want-or-force reply directly exposed the gap between acting on a desire and having selected the desire that governs action.","The consequential account preserved deterrence, prevention, rehabilitation, and compassion without relying on ultimate moral desert.","The evolutionary account supplied a naturalistic explanation for how moral restraint can remain motivationally effective under determinism."],"blunders":[{"text":"The case repeatedly treated the absence of ultimate self-creation as if it established the absence of every meaningful form of control, without defending that crucial bridge.","links":[{"label":"Begging the question","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"}]},{"text":"The source-regress argument generalized from upbringing, genetics, and conformity to all human behavior with insufficient qualification or comparative evidence.","links":[{"label":"Scope neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"}]}]},"con":{"score":82,"strengths":["The attributable-causation reply directly accepted determinism while preserving a clear distinction between compulsion and action issuing from the person.","The graded-voluntariness examples made freedom context-sensitive and operational rather than dependent on impossible self-creation.","Mediopassive agency offered a memorable model of conduct that both belongs to a person and arises through unmanufactured processes.","The interpersonal and normative replies preserved ethical answerability and moral meaning beyond causal or evolutionary description."],"blunders":[{"text":"The first-person prediction discussion complicated forecastability but did not establish that reflexive conduct escapes causal determination or answers the source regress.","links":[{"label":"Red herring","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/"}]},{"text":"The public-first consciousness reply moved between behavioral evidence and constitution of experience without directly answering the intention-to-movement mechanism challenge.","links":[{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest determinist critique should deny only ultimate desert: causally constituted agents retain practical capacities, but nobody ultimately authors the complete conditions from which those capacities and choices arise.","premises":["Every deliberative capacity, evaluative disposition, and operative desire depends partly on antecedent conditions that the agent did not ultimately create or select.","Reasons-responsive and voluntary action can constitute genuine practical control without granting responsibility for the complete causal formation of the controlling system.","Ultimate desert would require a stronger connection between agent and character than mere attribution, because attribution alone does not make the agent responsible for becoming that agent.","Forward-looking accountability can remain proportionate, evidence-sensitive, and respectful when justified by protection, repair, deterrence, rehabilitation, and interpersonal communication.","Institutions should therefore calibrate responses to demonstrated risk, capacity, harm, and prospects for change rather than to metaphysically ultimate blameworthiness."],"conclusion":"Determined people may act voluntarily and remain answerable, yet the unchosen formation of their agency defeats ultimate desert and supports humane, forward-looking responsibility practices."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The ownership–origination distinction","text":"Compatibilism persuasively shows that actions can belong to a person, but ownership and ultimate origination answer different questions. A decision may express the agent's values, deliberation, and reasons-responsive capacities while those very capacities arise from an unchosen developmental history. Ordinary attribution therefore supports prediction, dialogue, trust, and intervention without establishing that the agent deserves suffering simply because the complete causal history culminated in that person. This distinction preserves Carter's practical agency while sharpening O'Connor's narrower objection to ultimate desert."},{"title":"Institutional humility under causal uncertainty","text":"Even if metaphysical determinism remains unsettled, institutions know that behavior is strongly shaped by trauma, development, incentives, illness, opportunity, and social environment. Because decision-makers cannot cleanly isolate a person's ultimate contribution, punitive systems should adopt epistemic humility: require evidence of preventable harm, use the least destructive effective intervention, and continually reassess risk and rehabilitation. This policy does not depend on proving universal determinism; it follows from uncertainty about ultimate desert combined with the irreversible costs of excessive punishment."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest compatibilist view defines freedom as a graded capacity for reasons-responsive self-governance whose exercise is attributable to the person and appropriately sensitive to coercion, impairment, and context.","premises":["A useful concept of control distinguishes conduct responsive to the agent's reasons and evaluative capacities from seizures, coercion, manipulation, and incapacitating compulsion.","Causal determination does not erase that distinction, because causes operating through deliberative capacities differ systematically from causes bypassing or disabling those capacities.","Agency can be mediopassive: a thought, desire, or action may genuinely be mine although its enabling processes were neither transparent nor deliberately manufactured.","Responsibility should track the degree and quality of those capacities, including understanding, alternatives perceived, susceptibility to reasons, and freedom from controlling interference.","Interpersonal answerability can reject vindictive ultimate desert while retaining apology, justification, repair, trust revision, and recognition of victims and wrongdoers as persons."],"conclusion":"Determined agents can therefore possess meaningful degrees of freedom and responsibility when action issues through their reasons-responsive capacities without coercive bypass, even though nobody is ultimately self-created."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The bypass criterion","text":"The decisive contrast is not between caused and uncaused action but between causation through the person's evaluative system and causation that bypasses or disables it. Ordinary persuasion presents reasons that the agent can assess; coercion, neurological compulsion, and covert manipulation can suppress that assessment or make alternatives practically unavailable. Both kinds of events may be determined, yet they differ in the capacities exercised and in the counterfactual sensitivity of conduct to reasons. A freedom concept tracking that difference explains why responsibility varies without demanding self-creation."},{"title":"Responsibility as relational standing","text":"Responsibility is not only a prediction tool or a license to inflict suffering; it is a relational standing to ask for reasons, register injury, seek acknowledgment, and renegotiate trust. These practices presuppose capacities that admit degrees, not ultimate metaphysical independence. A person with impaired understanding may owe less explanation, while a competent person who knowingly violates shared expectations may owe more, even if both actions have complete causal histories. This model preserves compassion and rehabilitation while explaining why interpersonal responses cannot be reduced entirely to behavioral engineering."},{"title":"Counterfactual competence without alternate histories","text":"Freedom need not require that an identical universe could unfold differently. It can instead concern what the agent would do under relevant variations in reasons, information, opportunities, and constraints. Someone who would revise a decision after recognizing decisive evidence displays a competence absent in someone whose conduct is fixed by compulsion regardless of reasons. These counterfactual profiles are real properties of determined systems and support graded evaluations of agency. The proposal therefore preserves meaningful alternatives at the level of competence without asserting an unexplained break in causation."}]}},"year":2024};
